// sm-pmm — generated from src/sm-pmm-*.js by tools/merge.js. Edit the src files, then run: node tools/merge.js
// Product Marketing Manager. Format: [topic, question, answer, 'B'|'D', 'E'|'M'|'S'|'X', source, { t: top, f: [follow-up q, a] }]
QB["sm-pmm"] = (QB["sm-pmm"] || []).concat([
["Role", "What does a product marketing manager do?", "**Why they ask:** the role is often fuzzy.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I own how a product is positioned and brought to market: understanding the market and customers, defining positioning and messaging, leading launches, creating enablement so sales can sell, gathering competitive intelligence, and informing the product roadmap with market insight. I sit between product, sales and marketing, translating what we build into why customers should care. The core question I answer is: why this product, for whom, and why now.\"\n\nTip: Positioning, messaging, launch, enablement — the bridge role.", "B", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["PMM vs product manager?","PM decides what to build; PMM defines how to position, message and bring it to market."]}],
["Positioning", "How do you develop positioning for a product?", "**Why they ask:** the core PMM skill.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"From real customer and market understanding: who it's for, the problem it solves, the alternatives they'd otherwise use, and what makes us genuinely different and better for that audience. I pressure-test it against competitors and with customers and sales. Strong positioning is specific and true, not a list of features or vague superlatives. If it could describe any competitor, it's not positioning.\"\n\nTip: For whom, vs what alternative, uniquely why — specific and true.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["What makes positioning weak?","Being generic — 'the leading, easy-to-use platform' describes everyone and differentiates nothing."]}],
["Messaging", "How do you turn positioning into messaging?", "**Why they ask:** execution of positioning.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I build a messaging framework: the core value proposition, supporting pillars tied to customer pain and benefits, and proof points — then tailor it by audience and channel. I lead with the customer's problem and the value, not our features, and I test messaging with real customers and sales rather than guessing internally. Messaging that resonates uses the customer's language, not our internal jargon.\"\n\nTip: Benefit-led, audience-tailored, tested — customer's language.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Features or benefits — which leads?","Benefits and outcomes lead; features are proof of how you deliver them."]}],
["Launch", "How do you run a product launch?", "**Why they ask:** a signature PMM deliverable.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I define the launch goals and audience, the tier (how big a deal it is), the messaging, and a cross-functional plan — sales enablement, content, PR, campaigns, website, and internal readiness — with a timeline and owners. I make sure sales is trained and ready before we drive demand, and I measure launch outcomes against goals. A launch isn't the announcement; it's whether the market and sales actually adopt it.\"\n\nTip: Cross-functional plan; sales-ready before demand; measure adoption.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why ensure sales is ready before the launch?","Driving demand to an unprepared sales team wastes the interest and creates a bad buyer experience."]}],
["Enablement", "How do you enable the sales team?", "**Why they ask:** PMM makes sales effective.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I give sales what they need to win: clear messaging and talk tracks, pitch decks, battlecards against competitors, objection handling, demo guidance, and ROI tools — and I train them, not just hand over documents. I get feedback from the field on what's working and iterate. Great content nobody uses has no value, so I focus on adoption and real deal impact.\"\n\nTip: Train, don't just hand over; focus on adoption.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["What's a battlecard?","A quick-reference sheet on a competitor — their strengths, weaknesses, and how to position against them."]}],
["Competitive", "How do you do competitive intelligence?", "**Why they ask:** a key PMM function.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I track competitors' products, positioning, pricing and moves through their materials, win/loss analysis, customer and sales input, and analysts. I turn it into practical tools — battlecards and positioning — not just reports. The most valuable source is win/loss interviews: why we actually won or lost tells the real competitive story, beyond marketing claims.\"\n\nTip: Win/loss analysis is the richest competitive source.", "D", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["What do you learn from win/loss analysis?","The real reasons deals go our way or not — product gaps, pricing, positioning, or sales execution."]}],
["Research", "How do you understand your customers and market?", "**Why they ask:** foundation of PMM.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A mix of customer interviews, sales and CS input, win/loss, usage data, surveys, and market and analyst research. I build clear personas and understand the buying process — who's involved, what they care about, how they decide. Everything in PMM should trace back to real customer insight; positioning built from internal opinion instead of customer truth misses.\"\n\nTip: Ground everything in real customer insight.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["What's in a useful buyer persona?","Their goals, pains, how they evaluate, objections, and what messaging resonates — actionable, not demographic trivia."]}],
["Pricing", "How do you think about pricing and packaging?", "**Why they ask:** PMMs often inform pricing.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I ground it in customer value and willingness to pay, competitive context, and the behavior we want to drive, then structure packaging and tiers to match how different segments buy. I test and iterate rather than set it once. Pricing is one of the highest-leverage decisions, so I bring data and customer insight, not just cost-plus or gut.\"\n\nTip: Value-based, segment-aware, tested.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why is value-based pricing better than cost-plus?","It captures what the solution is worth to customers, which is usually far more than its cost to deliver."]}],
["Metrics", "How do you measure product marketing's impact?", "**Why they ask:** PMM impact can be fuzzy.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I tie to outcomes: launch adoption and revenue, sales enablement usage and win rates (especially competitive win rates), influence on pipeline, message resonance, and content effectiveness. I agree the measures with stakeholders upfront so impact is clear. PMM sits between teams, so I'm deliberate about showing contribution with real metrics rather than activity.\"\n\nTip: Win rates, adoption, pipeline influence — agreed upfront.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["How do you show PMM influenced a win?","Track competitive win rates before/after new battlecards and messaging, and get field feedback on what helped close deals."]}],
["Behavioral", "Tell me about a launch or repositioning you led.", "**Why they ask:** real experience.\n\n**Answer (example):**\n\"A product was underperforming because it was positioned on features in a crowded space. I ran customer interviews, found an underserved use case where we genuinely won, repositioned around it, rebuilt messaging and sales tools, and relaunched. Win rate and pipeline for that use case rose meaningfully. The repositioning, not the product, unlocked it.\"\n\nTip: Show insight-driven repositioning with a result.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["What was the key insight?","An underserved use case where our strengths genuinely mattered, versus competing head-on where we didn't stand out."]}],
["Collaboration", "How do you work with product and sales?", "**Why they ask:** the bridge role.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"With product, I bring market and customer insight to the roadmap and translate what's built into market value. With sales, I provide enablement and learn from the field what's really happening in deals. I'm the translator and connective tissue, so strong relationships and credibility with both are essential — I earn them by being genuinely useful to each.\"\n\nTip: Translator between product and sales; earn credibility by being useful.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["How do you influence the roadmap without owning it?","Bring compelling customer and competitive evidence so the PM wants the input, rather than demanding features."]}],
["Closing", "Why product marketing?", "**Why they ask:** motivation.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I love the mix of strategy, customer insight, storytelling and cross-functional impact — figuring out why a product matters and making the whole go-to-market work. It's where understanding customers turns into real business results, and I enjoy being that connective force.\"\n\nTip: Strategy + storytelling + cross-functional impact.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["What's your strongest PMM muscle?","Pick one — positioning, launches, enablement, or competitive — with an example."]}]
]);

// Product Marketing Manager. Format: [topic, question, answer, 'B'|'D', 'E'|'M'|'S'|'X', source, { t: top, f: [follow-up q, a] }]
QB["sm-pmm"] = (QB["sm-pmm"] || []).concat([
["Strategy", "What is a go-to-market strategy and what goes into one?", "**Why they ask:** GTM is the PMM's central deliverable.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A GTM strategy is the plan for how a product reaches and wins its market. It starts with who we're selling to — the target segment and ideal customer profile — and the problem we solve better than alternatives. Then it covers positioning and messaging, pricing and packaging, the sales motion or channels we'll use, and how we'll generate and convert demand. It ends with the metrics that tell us it's working. The thread through all of it is a clear point of view on the target customer and the value, because everything downstream, from pricing to the sales pitch, falls apart if that's fuzzy.\"\n\nTip: GTM = target, value, positioning, pricing, motion, metrics — anchored on a sharp customer and value.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["What's the most common GTM mistake?","Trying to be for everyone — a vague target leads to vague messaging and a sales team that can't tell a clear story about who it's for."]}],
["Research", "What's the difference between a buyer persona and an ideal customer profile?", "**Why they ask:** both guide targeting and messaging.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"An ICP describes the kind of company or account that's the best fit — things like industry, size, tech stack, and the triggers that make them need you. A buyer persona is a person within that account — their role, goals, pains, and what they care about in a decision. You use the ICP to decide who to target and the persona to decide how to talk to them, because the economic buyer, the user, and the technical evaluator in the same account need different messages. Getting both right means sales pursues the right accounts and says the right thing to each person in them.\"\n\nTip: ICP = which accounts to target; persona = how to speak to each person in them.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why do you need multiple personas for one sale?","Because a deal usually has several people — the user, the budget holder, the technical evaluator — and each weighs different things, so one message rarely moves all of them."]}],
["Positioning", "How do you know if your positioning is actually good?", "**Why they ask:** tests depth beyond writing a statement.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Good positioning is true, differentiated, and relevant to the customer, and the test is whether it holds up in the real world: does the sales team actually use it, do prospects nod and say 'that's us,' and does it win against the alternative they're really comparing you to. If reps default to their own pitch and ignore it, or customers don't see themselves in it, the positioning is wrong no matter how clever it sounds. I also check it against the true competitive alternative — including 'do nothing' — because positioning only matters relative to what the customer would otherwise choose.\"\n\nTip: Positioning works when sales uses it and customers recognize themselves — test it in the field.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why consider 'do nothing' as a competitor?","Because the most common deal you lose isn't to a rival — it's to the status quo, so your positioning has to beat inertia, not just other vendors."]}],
["Research", "What is win/loss analysis and how do you run it?", "**Why they ask:** win/loss is one of the highest-value PMM activities.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Win/loss is systematically learning why deals were won or lost by talking to the buyers — ideally a neutral interview, not just the rep's opinion, which is biased. I look for patterns across many deals: where we consistently win, where we lose and to whom, which objections recur, and whether it's product, pricing, positioning or sales execution. Those patterns feed real changes — messaging fixes, battlecard updates, pricing input, product feedback. The discipline is interviewing enough deals to see signal, and acting on it, rather than overreacting to one lost deal.\"\n\nTip: Interview the buyers for patterns, not the reps' opinions — then act on the patterns.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why not just ask the sales rep why they lost?","Reps have blind spots and incentives — they often blame price when the real issue was positioning or a missing capability; the buyer gives you the truer reason."]}],
["Launch", "How do you decide the tier or size of a product launch?", "**Why they ask:** not everything deserves a big launch.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I tier launches by impact and audience. A tier-one launch is a major product or capability that changes the market story — it gets the full treatment: press, analysts, campaigns, sales enablement, executive attention. A smaller feature might be a tier-two or three — a blog post, release notes, an in-app announcement, a note to sales. The mistake is treating every release like a mega-launch, which exhausts the audience and the team and dilutes the big moments. I match the investment to how much the thing actually matters to customers and the business.\"\n\nTip: Tier launches by impact — not every release deserves the full treatment.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["What's the risk of over-launching small features?","You train customers, press and sales to tune out your announcements, so the one that really matters gets ignored — launch fatigue is real."]}],
["Enablement", "What makes sales enablement content that reps actually use?", "**Why they ask:** most enablement content goes unused.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"It has to make the rep's next conversation easier, not add homework. That means it's built around what they're actually doing — a battlecard for a specific competitor, a one-page value story for a persona, a discovery guide, an objection-handling cheat sheet — not a 40-slide internal deck. I co-create it with top reps so it reflects how deals really go, keep it findable at the moment of need, and train on it rather than just posting it. And I measure adoption: if reps aren't using it, that's my signal to fix the content, not to blame the reps.\"\n\nTip: Build enablement around the rep's actual next conversation, co-create with top reps, measure adoption.", "D", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["How do you measure enablement effectiveness?","Adoption and outcomes — are reps using the materials, and do deals that use them progress or win better — not how many assets you produced."]}],
["Competitive", "How do you build and maintain a competitive battlecard?", "**Why they ask:** battlecards are a core PMM asset.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A good battlecard is short and field-ready: who the competitor is, where we win and why, where they win and how to handle it honestly, the traps to set and the objections to expect, with proof points. I build it from real win/loss data and field input, not just the competitor's website, and I keep it honest — overclaiming gets reps caught out and loses trust. I update it regularly because competitors move, and I make it findable in the flow of a deal. The test is whether a rep in a live deal reaches for it.\"\n\nTip: Short, honest, field-ready, updated — built from real win/loss, not the competitor's marketing.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why keep battlecards honest about competitor strengths?","Because reps hit those strengths in real deals — if the card pretends they don't exist, the rep gets blindsided and stops trusting it."]}],
["Pricing", "What's the difference between cost-plus and value-based pricing?", "**Why they ask:** pricing approach shapes the whole business.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Cost-plus prices off what it costs you to deliver plus a margin — simple, but it ignores what the product is worth to the customer and usually leaves money on the table or misprices. Value-based pricing starts from the value the customer gets — the problem solved, the money saved or made — and prices a fair share of that. It's harder because you have to quantify value and it varies by segment, but it aligns price with willingness to pay. For most software and differentiated products, value-based is the right frame; cost-plus is really for commodities.\"\n\nTip: Value-based prices to customer value and willingness to pay; cost-plus ignores both.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why is value-based pricing hard in practice?","You have to credibly quantify the value and it differs across segments, so it takes customer evidence and good packaging to capture it — but it's where the upside is."]}],
["Pricing", "How do you think about packaging and tiering?", "**Why they ask:** packaging drives revenue and adoption.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Packaging is how you bundle capabilities into offers, and good packaging makes the right choice obvious for each segment and creates a natural path to upgrade. I think about what each tier is for — a clear entry point, a main tier for the core buyer, and a premium tier — and which features are the 'value metric' that scales with how much a customer gets out of the product. The traps are too many confusing options, giving away the features that drive upgrades, and a value metric customers resent. Simple, aligned-to-value packaging beats clever complexity.\"\n\nTip: Make the right tier obvious, pick a fair value metric, and don't give away your upgrade drivers.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What's a value metric?","The unit you charge on that scales with the value a customer gets — seats, usage, records — chosen so paying more feels fair because they're getting more."]}],
["Analysts", "How do you work with industry analysts like Gartner or Forrester?", "**Why they ask:** analyst relations is often a PMM responsibility.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I treat analysts as an informed, skeptical audience who influence buyers, so the relationship is about genuinely informing them, not spinning. I keep them updated on our strategy and roadmap through briefings, respond thoughtfully to their evaluations like Magic Quadrants or Waves, and use their feedback as an honest outside read on our positioning and gaps. For evaluations, I make sure we present our real strengths clearly and back claims with evidence. They talk to many buyers and vendors, so they're also a great source of market intelligence if you listen as much as you pitch.\"\n\nTip: Inform analysts honestly and listen — they shape buyers and are a market-intel source.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why not just pitch analysts hard?","They see through spin and talk to your competitors and customers — credibility with them comes from substance, and overclaiming damages your standing in their evaluations."]}],
["Messaging", "How do you build a messaging framework or 'messaging house'?", "**Why they ask:** a framework keeps messaging consistent.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I structure it top-down: a core value proposition at the top — the single most important thing we do for the customer — supported by a few key message pillars, each backed by proof points and the features that deliver them. That gives everyone, from the website to a rep to a campaign, a consistent story at different levels of depth. I build it from customer language and value, not internal feature lists, and I keep it tight — three pillars people remember beat ten nobody does. The framework's job is consistency and focus across every touchpoint.\"\n\nTip: One value prop, a few proof-backed pillars, in customer language — consistency over completeness.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why limit it to a few pillars?","People remember three things, not ten — a focused message lands and gets repeated; an exhaustive one dilutes and gets forgotten."]}],
["Messaging", "How do you turn a feature into a benefit customers care about?", "**Why they ask:** feature-dumping is the classic PMM failure.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I run every feature through 'so what?' until I reach the customer outcome. A feature is what it does; the benefit is what that lets the customer achieve — time saved, risk reduced, revenue gained. For example, 'real-time sync' is a feature; the benefit is 'your team always works from the current numbers, so decisions aren't based on stale data.' I lead with the outcome and use the feature as the proof. Customers buy the result, not the mechanism, so my job is translating engineering's feature list into the value the buyer actually wants.\"\n\nTip: Ask 'so what?' until you reach the customer outcome — lead with that, prove it with the feature.", "D", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why do teams default to feature-dumping?","Because internally everyone is proud of the features and knows them cold — it takes discipline to translate to outcomes the customer, who doesn't care how it works, actually values."]}],
["Research", "How do you do voice-of-customer research to inform marketing and product?", "**Why they ask:** customer insight is the PMM's raw material.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I talk to customers directly — win/loss interviews, user interviews, advisory boards — and combine that with data like usage, support tickets, survey results and sales call recordings. I'm listening for the language they use, the jobs they're trying to get done, and the real pains behind feature requests. The skill is getting past what they say they want to the underlying need, because customers ask for faster horses. I synthesize it into insights the whole team can act on, and I bring the customer's actual words into messaging, because their language beats ours every time.\"\n\nTip: Combine direct interviews with usage and sales data; find the job behind the feature request.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What's 'jobs to be done'?","Framing what customers are really trying to accomplish — the progress they want — rather than the features they ask for, which reveals the true need and the real competition."]}],
["Launch", "What does launch readiness mean, and how do you ensure the company is ready?", "**Why they ask:** PMMs quarterback launches.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Launch readiness is every function being prepared for customers to actually use and buy the thing: the product works and is documented, sales can pitch and sell it, support can handle questions, marketing has the campaign and content, pricing is set, and success can onboard it. I run a cross-functional checklist and a readiness review, and I treat a genuine 'not ready' from support or sales as a real signal, not something to steamroll. A great product message means nothing if the field can't sell it or support can't handle the influx — internal readiness is half the launch.\"\n\nTip: Internal readiness — sales, support, pricing, docs — is half the launch, not an afterthought.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What's the most overlooked part of launch readiness?","Sales and support readiness — teams obsess over the external announcement and forget the people who'll actually sell and support it on day one."]}],
["Metrics", "How do you actually measure product marketing's impact?", "**Why they ask:** PMM impact is notoriously hard to attribute.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I tie it to outcomes PMM genuinely influences rather than claiming all of revenue. Launch success against its goals, sales enablement adoption and its effect on win rates and sales cycle, competitive win rate, content and messaging performance, and pipeline influenced by PMM-driven campaigns. Some of it is leading and qualitative — is the sales team telling a sharper story, are customers recognizing our positioning — and I'm honest about what I can and can't attribute. The goal is showing PMM moves the things it's responsible for, not taking credit for the whole funnel.\"\n\nTip: Measure what PMM truly influences — launch, enablement, win rate, competitive — not all of revenue.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why is PMM attribution hard?","PMM's impact is upstream and diffuse — it makes the whole funnel work better, so isolating its specific contribution from sales and demand gen is genuinely difficult."]}],
["Segmentation", "How do you decide which segment to focus a product or campaign on?", "**Why they ask:** focus is where PMM adds value.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I look at where we have the strongest fit and the best economics — segments where our value is sharpest, the buyers are reachable, deal sizes and retention are good, and we actually win. I'd rather dominate a well-defined segment than be mediocre everywhere, because focus lets messaging, content and the sales motion all reinforce each other. I use data on where we win and expand, plus market size and competitive intensity, to pick. Then I'm disciplined about saying no to tempting-but-distracting segments, because spreading thin is how good products stay stuck.\"\n\nTip: Win a well-defined segment before spreading — focus makes every other lever stronger.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["How do you push back on 'let's target everyone'?","Show that a focused segment wins more efficiently — concentrated messaging and references compound, while 'everyone' means generic messaging that converts no one well."]}],
["Collaboration", "How do you influence the product roadmap as a PMM?", "**Why they ask:** PMM is the market voice to product.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I earn influence by bringing evidence, not opinions — synthesized voice-of-customer, win/loss patterns, competitive gaps, and the revenue or deals tied to them — so product sees the market reality, not just my wish list. I frame requests around the customer problem and business impact rather than prescribing the feature, which respects product's ownership of the solution. I build a trusted partnership so they pull me in early, and I'm honest when something isn't a priority. The strongest PMM-product relationship is product wanting my market input, not me lobbying from outside.\"\n\nTip: Influence with evidence and customer problems, not a feature wish list — earn the partnership.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why frame it as a problem rather than a feature?","It respects product's job to design the solution and keeps the focus on the real customer need, which often has a better answer than the specific feature you'd have asked for."]}],
["Category", "What is category creation and when does it make sense?", "**Why they ask:** tests strategic marketing thinking.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Category creation is defining a new market category and positioning your product as the leader of it, rather than competing within an existing one. It can be powerful when your product genuinely does something new that existing categories don't describe, because owning the category shapes how buyers think and evaluate. But it's expensive and slow — you have to educate the whole market on a problem and a new way to solve it — and it fails if the 'new category' is really just your product with a fancy name. So it makes sense only with real differentiation and the resources to evangelize; otherwise, winning within a known category is faster.\"\n\nTip: Create a category only with genuine newness and resources to evangelize — otherwise win an existing one.", "D", "X", null, {"f":["What's the risk of forcing category creation?","Buyers don't understand or search for a category that doesn't resonate, so you spend heavily educating a market that would have found you faster inside an existing category."]}],
["Launch", "A launch underperformed. How do you figure out why and respond?", "**Why they ask:** launches fail and PMMs must diagnose.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I separate the possible failure points: was it awareness — did the target even hear about it; interest — did the message land; or conversion — did they hear it, like it, but not buy, which points to product, pricing or sales readiness. I look at the funnel metrics and talk to sales and customers to locate the break. Then I fix the specific stage rather than relaunching everything. I also check whether the goal was realistic in the first place. The discipline is diagnosing the actual failure point instead of concluding 'the launch flopped' and guessing.\"\n\nTip: Isolate awareness vs. message vs. conversion — fix the specific broken stage, not everything.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["Message landed but no one bought — what does that suggest?","A conversion problem downstream — pricing, product gaps, or sales not equipped to close — rather than a marketing message problem, so I'd look there."]}],
["Content", "How do you decide what marketing content to create?", "**Why they ask:** content can sprawl without strategy.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I start from the buyer's journey and the gaps in it — what does someone need to believe or learn at each stage to move forward, and where are we missing that. Early stage needs education on the problem; mid-stage needs proof we solve it, like case studies and comparisons; late stage needs objection-handling and ROI material. I prioritize content that unblocks real deals or stages where we lose people, and I'd rather make a few high-impact pieces that sales and customers actually use than a pile of blog posts. The journey and the data decide, not whoever shouts loudest.\"\n\nTip: Map content to buyer-journey gaps; make fewer pieces that unblock real stages.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["What content punches above its weight?","Strong case studies and honest competitive comparisons — late-stage buyers want proof and reassurance, and these directly move deals that are stuck on risk."]}],
["Enablement", "How do you run an effective sales kickoff or training session?", "**Why they ask:** PMMs often lead SKO content.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I design it around what reps need to do differently, not a one-way information dump. That means practicing the pitch and objection handling, not just listening to slides — reps retain what they rehearse. I keep it relevant to real deals, bring customer stories and proof, and make the takeaways findable afterward so it's not forgotten by next week. I also measure whether behavior actually changed, through call reviews or adoption of the new messaging. A great SKO is reps leaving able to tell a sharper story in their next call, not entertained for a day.\"\n\nTip: Reps retain what they rehearse — make them practice, keep it deal-relevant, measure the behavior change.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why isn't a great presentation enough for enablement?","People forget a one-way talk within days — adoption comes from practice, reinforcement, and findable materials, not from the quality of the slides."]}],
["Positioning", "How do you position against a much larger, better-known competitor?", "**Why they ask:** underdog positioning is common.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I don't try to beat them at their own game — I change the terms. I find where their size is actually a weakness: slower, more complex, built for a different buyer, bloated, or expensive, and I own the opposite where it's true for our target. I get specific about the segment or use case where we genuinely win, rather than claiming to be better at everything, which no one believes from a smaller player. And I use focus and agility as real advantages. The goal is making the comparison be on dimensions where we win, not on the ones where their brand and scale dominate.\"\n\nTip: Change the terms of comparison to where your focus wins — don't fight the giant on its strengths.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why not just claim you're better across the board?","Buyers don't believe a smaller vendor is better at everything — a credible, specific 'better for you, in this way' beats an unbelievable 'better than them, period.'"]}],
["Research", "What is TAM, SAM, and SOM and why do they matter?", "**Why they ask:** market sizing informs strategy and focus.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Total Addressable Market is the whole market demand if everyone who could buy did; Serviceable Available Market is the slice your product and model can actually serve; and Serviceable Obtainable Market is the realistic share you can win in a given period. They matter for prioritization and for honest planning — a huge TAM is meaningless if your SAM is tiny or your SOM is unrealistic. I use them to pick where to focus and to set grounded expectations, and I'm wary of inflated top-down TAM numbers that justify a strategy rather than inform it.\"\n\nTip: TAM is the dream, SAM is what you can serve, SOM is what you'll realistically win — plan on SOM.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["What's the danger of leading with a giant TAM?","It can justify an unfocused strategy and set expectations you can't meet — the obtainable share, SOM, is the number that should drive real decisions."]}],
["Collaboration", "How do you handle a conflict with product management over the roadmap or messaging?", "**Why they ask:** PMM-product friction is common.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I start from shared goals and bring data to depersonalize it — win/loss, customer evidence, competitive gaps — so we're debating the market, not our egos. I respect that product owns the solution and the roadmap, while I own the market read and the story, and most conflicts dissolve when each side trusts the other's domain. If we still disagree on something material, I frame the trade-off clearly and escalate to a shared leader for a decision rather than letting it fester. A strong, trusting PMM-product relationship is one of the biggest predictors of a product's market success.\"\n\nTip: Bring data, respect each other's domain, escalate real trade-offs cleanly — don't let it fester.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What undermines the PMM-product relationship fastest?","PMM lobbing feature demands without evidence, or product ignoring market reality — both break trust; evidence and respect for each other's ownership rebuild it."]}],
["Behavioral", "Tell me about a launch or repositioning you led and what you learned.", "**Why they ask:** demonstrates real PMM experience.\n\n**Answer (example):**\n\"I led a repositioning for a product that was being sold as a generic tool and losing on price. Through customer interviews I found a specific segment where it solved a painful, high-value problem no competitor framed well. I rebuilt the positioning and messaging around that, re-enabled sales with a focused story and battlecard, and we started winning those deals at better prices. The lesson was that the product didn't need to change — it needed a sharper point of view about who it was really for and why that mattered. Clarity beat features.\"\n\nTip: Show a sharper point-of-view driving a measurable result — ideally positioning, not product, did the work.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["How did you validate the new positioning before rolling it out?","Tested it in real customer and prospect conversations and with a few trusted reps, and checked it won against the true alternative, before committing the whole field to it."]}],
["Behavioral", "Describe a time you had to say no to a stakeholder's request.", "**Why they ask:** PMMs are pulled in every direction.\n\n**Answer (example):**\n\"Sales wanted me to cram a long feature list into the pitch, convinced more features would win. I pushed back with win/loss data showing we lost on a clear value story, not on feature count, and proposed a focused message with the features as proof points behind it. I didn't just refuse — I showed why and offered a better path. The focused pitch performed better, and it built credibility for the next time I had to say no. Saying no well is a big part of PMM, because the job is focus, and everyone wants their thing added.\"\n\nTip: Say no with evidence and a better alternative, not just refusal — focus is the PMM's job.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why is saying no central to product marketing?","Because everyone wants their feature or segment emphasized, and a message that includes everything persuades no one — protecting focus is where PMM creates value."]}],
["Scenario", "Sales says your messaging doesn't work in real deals. How do you respond?", "**Why they ask:** tests humility and field connection.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I take it seriously and go find out, rather than defending the messaging. I listen to real calls or ride along on deals, talk to reps and ideally customers, and figure out whether it's a messaging problem, an enablement problem, or reps reverting to old habits. If the message genuinely isn't landing, I fix it — the field is closer to the customer than my doc is. If it's an adoption or training gap, I address that. Either way, 'sales won't use my messaging' is feedback about me, not a sales failing, and I treat it that way.\"\n\nTip: Go listen to real deals — if the field won't use the message, that's feedback on the message.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["What if it's a mix of both bad messaging and poor adoption?","Fix the message with field input first so it's genuinely usable, then enable and reinforce adoption — you can't drive adoption of something that doesn't actually work in deals."]}],
["Demand gen", "How do you collaborate with demand generation?", "**Why they ask:** PMM and demand gen must align.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I own the 'what and why' — the positioning, messaging, target and offers — and demand gen owns the 'how and where' — the channels, campaigns and mechanics to generate and convert pipeline. We work tightly: I give them the message and the audience insight, they tell me what's resonating and converting so I can sharpen the message with real data. The failure mode is PMM handing over a message and disappearing, or demand gen running campaigns off-message. We stay joined up on the funnel, from the message down to what actually converts.\"\n\nTip: PMM owns the message and audience; demand gen owns channels and mechanics — stay joined up on results.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["What does demand gen give back to PMM?","Real conversion data by message and channel, which tells me what's actually resonating so I can refine positioning with evidence rather than opinion."]}],
["Pricing", "A sales team wants to discount heavily to close deals. How do you weigh in?", "**Why they ask:** PMM influences pricing discipline.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I'd look at whether discounting is a symptom of a value problem — if we're constantly cutting price, often the real issue is that we're not communicating value well enough, or we're targeting the wrong buyer who doesn't feel the pain. So I'd dig into win/loss on price-lost deals before just blessing bigger discounts. Tactically, discounts can be fine with guardrails and something in return, like a longer term, but habitual deep discounting erodes margin and trains the market to wait for a deal. I'd push to fix the value story first, then control discounting with policy.\"\n\nTip: Chronic discounting is usually a value-communication problem — fix the story before cutting price.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why is habitual discounting dangerous?","It erodes margin, anchors customers to the lower price, and trains buyers to wait for a discount — it masks a value or targeting problem instead of solving it."]}],
["Research", "How do you quickly get up to speed on a market and product you're new to?", "**Why they ask:** PMMs change products and must ramp fast.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I go to the sources of truth fast: talk to customers and lost prospects, listen to sales calls, use the product myself, and talk to sales, support and product. I read the competitors' positioning and the analyst view, and I look at the win/loss and usage data. Within a few weeks I want a clear point of view on who the best-fit customer is, the real value, who we compete with, and where we win and lose. I prioritize primary sources — actual customers and deals — over internal decks, because the internal story is often outdated or wishful.\"\n\nTip: Go straight to customers, calls, and the product — primary sources beat internal decks.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["What's the first thing you'd want to understand?","Who our best-fit customer really is and the specific value they get — everything else, from messaging to pricing, depends on nailing that."]}],
["Content", "How do you build a customer reference and case study program?", "**Why they ask:** proof points are among PMM's most valuable assets.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I treat references as a pipeline, not a scramble the day sales needs one. I identify happy customers early — often via success teams and NPS — and build a mix of written case studies, quotes, logos and reference calls at different depths so I always have something ready. For each, I focus the story on the customer's problem and measurable outcome, not our features, because buyers trust a peer's result over our marketing. I make references easy to find and I protect the relationship — never over-ask a reference until they burn out. A strong proof library moves late-stage deals more than almost anything else.\"\n\nTip: Build references as a pipeline with real outcomes — peer proof beats vendor claims.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why focus a case study on the customer, not the product?","Buyers relate to a peer's problem and result and trust it more than a feature pitch — the product is the supporting character, not the hero."]}],
["Launch", "How do you run a beta or early access program before a launch?", "**Why they ask:** betas de-risk launches and build proof.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A beta serves two goals: validating the product works for real users and generating evidence and references for launch. I recruit the right early customers — engaged, representative of the target, willing to give feedback — set clear expectations about what's unfinished, and create tight feedback loops. I watch for whether they actually adopt and get value, which is the real signal, not just that they signed up. The outputs feed the launch: fixed issues, refined messaging based on how they describe the value, and early proof points and quotes. A good beta means launching with evidence instead of hope.\"\n\nTip: A beta validates value and generates launch proof — recruit the right users and watch real adoption.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["What's the key signal to watch in a beta?","Whether users actually adopt it and get value in their real workflow — enthusiasm in a survey means little if they don't keep using it."]}],
["Messaging", "How do you craft a strong elevator pitch or tagline for a product?", "**Why they ask:** distilling the story is a core PMM skill.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I compress the positioning to its essence: for whom, the problem, and the unique value, in language a human would actually say. A good elevator pitch makes someone in the target go 'oh, I need that' in one breath. I avoid jargon and buzzwords that sound impressive but say nothing, and I test it on real people — if they can repeat it back and it's accurate, it works. A tagline is even tighter and more emotional, but it still has to be true to the positioning. The hard part is ruthless subtraction, not clever wording.\"\n\nTip: Compress to for-whom, problem, unique value in human language — then test if people can repeat it.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["How do you know a pitch is working?","When target customers repeat it back accurately and lean in — if they look confused or can't restate it, it's too clever or too vague."]}],
["Launch", "How important is internal launch communication, and how do you handle it?", "**Why they ask:** internal alignment makes or breaks launches.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"It's as important as the external launch and more often neglected. Before anything goes public, I make sure everyone internal — sales, support, success, execs, even other teams — knows what's launching, why it matters, the key messages, and what they need to do. I tailor it: sales needs the pitch and enablement, support needs FAQs and troubleshooting, execs need the strategic story and talking points. The worst launch outcome is a customer asking a rep about the new thing and the rep not knowing it exists. Internal readiness and alignment are the quiet backbone of a smooth launch.\"\n\nTip: Align every internal team before going public — a rep caught unaware is a launch failure.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["What's a simple way to keep internal teams aligned at launch?","A clear internal launch brief and a short all-hands or enablement session, plus findable FAQs — so no one customer-facing is caught off guard."]}],
["Strategy", "What's the difference between product-led and sales-led growth, and how does it change your job?", "**Why they ask:** the motion shapes PMM priorities.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"In sales-led growth, a sales team drives deals, so PMM invests heavily in enablement, pitches, battlecards and supporting the human sale. In product-led growth, the product itself acquires and converts users — think free trials or freemium — so PMM shifts toward in-product messaging, onboarding, activation, and the self-serve journey, because there's no rep to carry the story; the product and the UI have to do it. Many companies blend both. The core PMM skills are the same, but where I apply positioning and messaging moves from a sales deck to the product experience itself.\"\n\nTip: Sales-led PMM arms reps; product-led PMM shapes onboarding and in-product messaging.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What matters most in product-led growth?","Activation — getting a new user to their first real value fast — because in PLG the product's onboarding, not a salesperson, is what converts, so reducing time-to-value is everything."]}],
["Expansion", "How do you think about messaging for expansion and retention, not just acquisition?", "**Why they ask:** growth increasingly comes from the installed base.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Most durable growth comes from keeping and expanding existing customers, yet PMM energy often skews to acquisition. For retention, I focus on reinforcing value — helping customers see the outcomes they're getting so they renew — and on messaging new features to existing users so they adopt more. For expansion, I craft the story for cross-sell and upsell: the next problem we can solve for a customer who already trusts us. I partner with customer success on this, because the expansion message lands best from a trusted success relationship, not a cold pitch. Retention messaging is lower-glamour but higher-leverage.\"\n\nTip: Reinforce realized value for retention and message the 'next problem' for expansion — it's higher-leverage than acquisition.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why partner with customer success on expansion?","They own the trusted relationship and see usage, so an expansion message delivered through success lands far better than a cold upsell from marketing or sales."]}],
["Objections", "How do you equip sales to handle common objections?", "**Why they ask:** objection handling wins or loses deals.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I gather the real objections from the field and win/loss — price, a missing feature, 'why not the incumbent,' risk of switching — and build honest, tested responses for each, not spin. For price, I arm reps to reframe to value and ROI; for a feature gap, I give an honest acknowledgment plus the workaround or roadmap context; for risk, proof points and references. I train reps to practice these, because a response read off a card unconvincingly is worse than none. The principle is honesty — a response that overclaims gets the rep caught and loses trust mid-deal.\"\n\nTip: Build honest, practiced responses from real field objections — overclaiming backfires in the deal.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["How do you handle an objection about a genuine product gap?","Acknowledge it honestly, give the workaround or roadmap context, and redirect to where our overall value still wins — pretending the gap doesn't exist destroys credibility."]}],
["Scenario", "You inherit a product with messaging that's all features and no clear story. Where do you start?", "**Why they ask:** a very common real PMM situation.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I start with customers, not the messaging doc. I'd interview current customers and recent wins and losses to learn why people actually buy, what value they get, and the language they use, plus listen to sales calls. From that I'd form a clear point of view on the best-fit customer and the core value, draft tight positioning and a few message pillars, and pressure-test them with trusted reps and prospects before rolling out. Then I'd cascade it — website, sales enablement, content — so the whole company tells one story. The fix isn't clever copy; it's the clarity underneath that the feature-dump was hiding.\"\n\nTip: Start with customers to find the real value, then build clarity underneath — copy comes last.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why not just rewrite the messaging directly?","Because polished copy on top of unclear positioning is still unclear — you have to establish who it's for and the core value first, or you're just rearranging feature lists."]}],
["Closing", "What separates a great product marketer from an average one?", "**Why they ask:** tests understanding of the craft.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"An average PMM produces decks and content; a great one has a sharp, evidence-based point of view about the customer and the market, and gets the whole company to act on it. The difference is clarity and influence — turning messy market signal into a crisp story that sales tells, product builds toward, and customers recognize themselves in. It comes from being genuinely close to customers and deals, not just internal meetings, and from the discipline to focus when everyone wants to add more. Great PMM is strategic clarity made usable by everyone who touches the customer.\"\n\nTip: Great PMMs turn market signal into a sharp point of view the whole company acts on.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Where does that point of view come from?","Being close to real customers and deals — win/loss, calls, interviews — not from internal decks; the clarity is earned from primary market contact."]}]
]);
